"OUT-OF-ROUND CONCESSIONS -- after the COLLISION POINTS lines, add a section titled exactly OUT-OF-ROUND CONCESSIONS, the title on its own line. List one line for each concession either side made OUTSIDE the two link rounds -- that is, ground granted in [CR1], [DR1], [CR2] or [DR2], not in [CR3] or [DR3]. Form, one per line: SIDE | ROUND | the verbatim sentence in which the ground was granted, where SIDE is DEFENDER or CHALLENGER. If there are none, write exactly: NONE. BINDING: these are recorded separately and DO NOT ENTER CONCEDED LINKS, CHALLENGER CONCEDED LINKS, DEFENSE STANDING, CHALLENGE STANDING, the CLAUSE line or the top-line verdict. The counting rule above is unchanged and still governs those lines: a premise counts as CONCEDED only from the link round block itself. Never sum an out-of-round concession with a link-round count."+NL+NL+"DEFENDER CHAIN MARK: <FOOTED|STRAINED|SLACK> -- <one clause naming the load-bearing link that decides the mark>."+NL+"CHALLENGER CHAIN MARK: <FOOTED|STRAINED|SLACK> -- <one clause naming the load-bearing link that decides the mark>."+NL+"These two lines come last, after OUT-OF-ROUND CONCESSIONS, the defender's first. One definition marks each side's own constructed chain, the defender's declared chain (D1 to the conclusion link) and the challenger's own alternative reading: FOOTED = every load-bearing link of the side's chain is footed on an exhibited L1-L3 layer; its reading stands as a built structure. STRAINED = the chain is built but one or more load-bearing links float, asserted at L4 without exhibited footing; it stands only under charity. SLACK = the side advanced no coherent chain of its own: objections without a constructed reading, or a construction that never cohered. Each mark describes ONLY how well that side built its own chain. It is descriptive, not a verdict. It MUST NOT change DEFENSE STANDING, CHALLENGE STANDING, CONCEDED LINKS, CHALLENGER CONCEDED LINKS, the CLAUSE line, KEYSTONE or the top-line verdict. A FOOTED chain is fully compatible with its side losing the debate; a SLACK chain is fully compatible with its side winning it."
